<TopicPage slug="subjects/petroleum-engineering/foundational/petroleum-geology-and-reservoir-rock">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Petroleum engineering begins as geology, because petroleum exists only where a specific
    coincidence of geological circumstances happened: a mature <strong>source rock</strong> that
    generated oil or gas, a <strong>migration path</strong> that carried it, a
    <strong>reservoir rock</strong> porous enough to store it, a <strong>seal</strong> that
    stopped it escaping, and <strong>timing</strong> — the trap must have formed before the
    hydrocarbons arrived. Miss any one of the five and the result is not a smaller field; it is
    no field at all. Then the discipline becomes engineering, because what matters next are two
    rock properties — <strong>porosity</strong> (how much fluid the rock stores) and
    <strong>permeability</strong> (how fast it flows) — and one law, Darcy's, connecting them to
    production. This page walks the system in that order: where oil comes from, where it stops,
    what kind of rock holds it, and what kind of fluids fill it.
  </p>
  <p>
    One reframing helps everything else: an oil field is not a lake or a cavern. It is
    ordinary rock — sandstone or limestone — whose microscopic pore spaces happen to be full of
    hydrocarbons, held a few kilometres underground at elevated temperature and pressure, with
    water below and often gas above. Producing it means persuading fluids to flow through
    pores measured in microns, across distances measured in kilometres, against physics that
    would much rather leave them in place. Every piece of petroleum engineering is a
    negotiation with that physics.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="source, migration, trap, storage, flow" widget="equationStep" config={reservoirCfg} />

  <h2>Sedimentary basins and traps: making oil, then catching it</h2>
  <p>
    Petroleum is solar energy in chemical storage: ancient marine plankton and algae photosynthesised,
    settled into oxygen-poor basins where they could not fully decay, and were buried in mud
    that became organic-rich <strong>source rock</strong> (black shale). Burial is the cooking.
    As sediments pile kilometres deep, temperature climbs roughly 25–30 °C per kilometre, and
    between about 60 and 120 °C the solid organic matter — <strong>kerogen</strong> — cracks
    thermally into liquid hydrocarbons: this is the <strong>oil window</strong>. Push deeper,
    past roughly 120–150 °C, and the cracking continues to wet gas and then dry gas — the
    <strong>gas window</strong>. Too shallow, and the kitchen never lit; too deep, and the oil
    overcooked to gas (or graphite). This is why geologists study <strong>sedimentary
    basins</strong> — the long-lived depressions where kilometres of sediment accumulated
    (rift basins, passive continental margins, foreland basins beside mountain belts) — because
    basins are kitchens: they alone provide the burial history that generates petroleum.
  </p>
  <p>
    Newly generated oil is lighter than the brine saturating the rocks around it (oil roughly
    0.8–0.9 g/cm³, formation water 1.0–1.1), so <strong>buoyancy drives migration</strong> —
    first out of the tight source rock itself (primary migration, still partly debated), then
    upward and laterally through any permeable "carrier bed" (secondary migration), in a
    process that can cover tens of kilometres. Migration is aimless upward wandering until
    something stops it: a <strong>trap</strong>. The classic trap is structural — an
    <strong>anticline</strong>, an upward fold whose crest gathers buoyant fluids like an
    upside-down cup; faults can seal too, smearing clay along the slip plane. The other family
    is stratigraphic — a sandstone body <em>pinching out</em> updip into shale, a fossil
    <strong>reef</strong> of porous carbonate ringed by tight mudrock, an unconformity surface
    capping tilted beds. In every case the working parts are the same: porous rock below,
    impermeable <strong>seal</strong> above (shale or, best of all, rock salt), and the fluids
    arranged by density — gas at the top, oil beneath it, water at the bottom, the
    <em>hydrocarbon column</em>. The giant fields that anchor national economies (Ghawar in
    Saudi Arabia is the canonical anticline example) are simply places where all five
    petroleum-system elements coincided, on a scale of billions of barrels.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> that oil resides in underground lakes or rivers. Reservoirs are solid rock saturated with fluids; there is no "pool" to pump dry, only pore space to drain. This is why production is measured as a gradual decline rather than a level dropping — the well is draining rock, cubic metre by cubic metre, from kilometres around.</p>

  <h2>Porosity and permeability: the tank and the plumbing</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Porosity</strong> (φ) is the fraction of the rock's volume that is void: φ = pore
    volume ÷ bulk volume, quoted as a percentage. A good sandstone runs 15–30 per cent;
    carbonates vary wildly because their pores include vugs, fossil moulds and fractures as
    well as interparticle space. Porosity is controlled by the depositional story and its
    history: well-sorted beach sands keep their pores; poor-sorted muds bury theirs;
    compaction squeezes pores shut with depth; cement (quartz and calcite precipitating in
    pore throats) destroys them; dissolution can reopen them (secondary porosity). Porosity is
    the <em>tank</em>: a 100 m-thick, 20 per cent-porous reservoir section stores roughly
    200,000 litres of fluid under every square metre of area — the arithmetic behind every
    reserves calculation.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Permeability</strong> (k) is the property that separates a reservoir from a merely
    porous rock: how easily fluid flows through the connected pore network. Its unit, the
    darcy, is defined so that practical rock falls in readable numbers: a clean, well-connected
    sandstone runs 0.1–10 darcies (100–10,000 millidarcies, mD); a "tight" gas sand might be
    0.01–1 mD; the organic shales of the unconventional revolution measure in the
    <strong>nano- to microdarcy</strong> range — a million to a billion times less permeable
    than a good sand, effectively sealed. The two properties are related but <em>not</em>
    coupled: pumice is spectacularly porous and impermeable (disconnected bubbles); a fractured
    granite can be nearly zero-porosity and flow like a pipe. What couples them is pore-throat
    size and connectedness — which is why a rock's permeability depends steeply on the small
    fraction of its largest pores. The defining relation is <strong>Darcy's law</strong>:
    flow rate = permeability × area × pressure drop ÷ (viscosity × length). It reads as the
    profession's menu of leverage: you cannot change the rock, but you <em>can</em> increase
    the pressure drawdown (bigger pumps, lower bottom-hole pressure), attack viscosity (heat
    the oil with steam, dilute with solvent), or manufacture permeability — hydraulic
    fracturing propping open artificial fractures, horizontal wells intersecting kilometres of
    pay. The widget below lets you feel each lever: drag permeability from 10 to 1000 mD at
    fixed pressure and the flow multiplies a hundredfold; drag viscosity from 0.5 to 50 cP and
    it collapses by the same factor — the entire contrast between a light-oil field and a
    heavy-oil one, in three sliders.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="flow response to k, ΔP and viscosity" widget="sliderLab" config={darcyCfg} />
  <p>
    Real reservoirs add two refinements. <strong>Two phases compete</strong>: when oil and
    water (or gas) share the pores, each obstructs the other — relative permeability curves
    show oil flowing at only a fraction of its single-phase rate as water saturation rises,
    which is why water production strangles oil wells. And <strong>capillary forces</strong>
    hold water on grain surfaces and control how far buoyant oil can force its way past a
    seal's pore throats — capillary entry pressure is the physics that makes a seal a seal.
  </p>

  <h2>Reservoir fluids: what is down there and how it comes out</h2>
  <p>
    The fluids are classified by composition and phase behaviour. <strong>Black oil</strong>
    (the classic medium-gravity crude) dissolves modest gas; <strong>volatile oils</strong>
    carry much more; <strong>gas condensates</strong> exist as gas underground but drop
    valuable liquids as pressure falls; <strong>dry gas</strong> is nearly pure methane.
    Gravity is graded on the API scale (°API = 141.5/SG − 131.5): light crudes around 35–45 °API
    flow easily and refine richly; heavy oils below 20 °API are syrupy (viscosity in the tens
    to thousands of centipoise) and demand steam or dilution to move at all. Pressure and
    temperature matter as much as composition: as a reservoir is produced and pressure falls
    toward the <strong>bubble point</strong>, gas comes out of solution in the pores — good for
    energy, bad for the oil's flow properties.
  </p>
  <p>
    That pressure energy is the production story. <strong>Primary recovery</strong> rides the
    reservoir's own drive mechanisms — dissolved gas expanding (solution-gas drive), a gas cap
    pushing down, an aquifer pushing up — and recovers typically 5–20 per cent of the oil in
    place before the energy is spent. <strong>Secondary recovery</strong> injects water to
    maintain pressure and sweep oil toward producers (waterflooding), lifting cumulative
    recovery toward 30–50 per cent in good reservoirs. <strong>Enhanced oil recovery</strong>
    attacks the physics directly: steam injection thins heavy oil, CO<sub>2</sub> injected
    above its critical condition mixes with and swells the oil while freeing trapped
    ganglia, chemical floods lower the water–oil interfacial tension that traps blobs of oil
    in pore throats. Even then, roughly half the oil typically stays underground — the
    industry's standing challenge. Reserves arithmetic follows from the pore scale upward:
    oil in place = area × thickness × porosity × (1 − water saturation) ÷ oil volume factor,
    with the uncertainty expressed as P90/P50/P10 cases — a geology that arrives as
    probabilities, not answers.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    One worked calculation shows how the pore-scale arithmetic scales to a field. Take a
    reservoir 2,000 acres in area, 50 ft thick, 20 per cent porous, with connate water filling
    25 per cent of the pore space and an oil volume factor of 1.2 (each stock-tank barrel of
    oil occupied 1.2 reservoir barrels underground). Oil initially in place is area ×
    thickness × porosity × (1 − water saturation) ÷ volume factor — in field units,
    7758 barrels per acre-foot per unit of the product: 7758 × 2,000 × 50 × 0.20 × 0.75 ÷ 1.2
    ≈ 97 million stock-tank barrels. Every factor is an estimate with its own uncertainty —
    porosity from logs calibrated to cores, saturation from resistivity, volume factor from
    laboratory PVT analysis — which is why the answer is quoted as P90/P50/P10 cases rather
    than a number, and why a 15 per cent recovery factor (about 14.5 million barrels here)
    is itself a moving target improved by every waterflood and infill well. The reservoir
    engineer's career is spent shrinking the error bars on that one multiplication.
  </p>
  <p>
    Three deeper threads run from this foundation into modern practice. <strong>Petrophysics
    at a distance</strong>: nobody sees a reservoir; its properties arrive as inference.
    Well logs — gamma ray distinguishing shale from sand, resistivity revealing brine versus
    hydrocarbon (Archie's law converts resistivity to water saturation), density and neutron
    porosity — build the model; core samples calibrate it; pressure transient analysis tests
    it; and seismic imaging extends it between wells, where most of the uncertainty lives.
    <strong>The unconventional revolution as a porosity–permeability story</strong>: shale
    source rocks are rich but nano-permeable, so for a century they were only ever sources —
    until horizontal drilling plus multi-stage hydraulic fracturing manufactured the missing
    permeability at scale in the 2000s–2010s, redrawing the global supply map. The lesson is
    the discipline's: the resource was never missing; the plumbing was. The same physics
    explains the economics (decline curves fall steeply as fracture networks drain fast then
    starve) and the environmental agenda (water use, induced seismicity, methane leakage).
    <strong>The portable skill set</strong>: reservoir engineering is fundamentally the
    management of fluids in porous media under pressure — and that competency transfers
    directly to geologic CO<sub>2</sub> storage in saline aquifers (inject, contain, monitor),
    to compressed-air and hydrogen storage in depleted fields, and to enhanced geothermal
    systems, which are literally hydraulic fracturing for heat. Modern petroleum curricula
    (and the ABET criteria behind them) therefore pair Darcy's law with safety, economics and
    energy-transition literacy: the rock mechanics are identical, and the mission is widening.
    The foundation, though, stays what it has always been — geology to find it, rock physics
    to produce it, and the honesty to know which of the two you are currently wrong about.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> ABET petroleum program criteria; Ahmed, <em>Reservoir Engineering Handbook</em>; Tiab &amp; Donaldson, <em>Petrophysics</em>.</p>
